{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795170219,"nonce":"kz/8t991+4COMPaeFuDmkynlBfE=","title":"An adaptive remesh of a P2 problem returns a vertex-sized solution","message":"fem.solve(adapt=jno.solve.remesh(...)) on a problem whose unknown has order=2 returns, after the first remesh round, a solution with one value per mesh vertex instead of one per P2 DOF, and the error against the exact solution grows instead of falling.\n\nReproduction: Laplace on the L-shaped domain (polygon (0,0),(1,0),(1,.5),(.5,.5),(.5,1),(0,1), mesh size 0.1), boundary data u = r^(2/3) sin(2 phi/3) at the re-entrant corner, u, phi = d.fem_symbols(order=2), fem.solve(adapt=jno.solve.remesh(theta=0.5, max_iters=k, refine_factor=2.0)).\n\n  k=0: 417 values, L2 error 7.1e-4 (correct)\n  k=1: 115 values (the vertex count), L2 error 1.8e-1\n  k=2: 136 values, L2 error 3.4e-1\n\nThe same script with order=1 gives 115, 115, 136 values and L2 errors 3.3e-3, 3.3e-3, 1.6e-3. fem.dofs still reports the P2 count after the remesh, so the mismatch is silent: fem.eval reads the vertex-sized vector as a P2 field. Expected: the remeshed problem keeps the element order, or the remesh raises for an order it does not support.","files":null}]}